#
# NEXTBSD-RPI5 -- Raspberry Pi 5 family (BCM2712 + RP1).
#
# Covers the Pi 5 B, the Pi 500 and 500+, and CM5: same SoC, same southbridge,
# same debug UART at the same address. Nothing in this file is specific to one
# of them.
#
# COVERAGE: only the Pi 500+ has ever been booted. The rest is claimed on
# shared silicon and a config with no board-specific content in it, which is a
# good reason to expect it to work and not the same thing as having seen it.
# A board that is not the 500+ is worth treating as a first boot: read the
# serial log, do not assume.
#
# Identical to NEXTBSD except that it can talk. These boards have no UEFI and
# no simple-framebuffer node, so serial is the only console.
#
# SOCDEV_PA is UART10, the PL011 inside the BCM2712, reached through the 3-pin
# JST-SH debug connector on the board. Confirmed on the hardware: Linux reports
# `107d001000.serial: ttyAMA10 ... is a PL011 rev3`, and the serial10 alias
# resolves to /soc@107c000000/serial@7d001000 -- soc@, not pcie@.
#
# This was 0x1c00030000 (RP1 UART0, the 40-pin header) until that connector was
# found, and moving it is permanent rather than a bring-up convenience. RP1 is
# a PCIe endpoint, and console attach happens at cninit(), long before any PCIe
# host bridge has attached -- so the 40-pin UART can never be a boot console on
# these boards no matter how complete bcm2712-pcie and rp1 become. It can be a
# late tty; it cannot be the console. It also dies the moment a driver owns the
# controller, which during the RP1 work makes a working driver look exactly
# like a dead one. UART10 is in the SoC, is at the same address on every board
# in the family, and is audible earlier besides: it carries the boot ROM and
# SDRAM training, which the header never saw.
#
# locore.S maps one 2MB device block at SOCDEV_PA and keeps the offset within
# it, so the exact register address is the right value to give here.
#
include NEXTBSD

ident		NEXTBSD-RPI5

options 	SOCDEV_PA=0x107d001000
options 	EARLY_PRINTF=pl011

# A real console for the whole boot, not just the early window. SOCDEV_PA above
# only covers printf before cninit(), and socdev_va stops being valid once the
# early pagetables go. See config/rpi5.env for why each tag is required.
env		"rpi5.env"

# Turn off the wrong-SoC Broadcom drivers.
#
# arm64 GENERIC includes std.broadcom, which sets SOC_BRCM_BCM2837 and
# SOC_BRCM_BCM2838 -- the Pi 3 and Pi 4 SoCs. This board is BCM2712, which is
# neither, but the Pi 500 DTB carries compatible strings close enough that
# those drivers match anyway and then panic on hardware they do not describe.
#
# Measured on the board: bcm2835_fbd attaches, asks the VideoCore mailbox for
# the framebuffer dimensions, and panics one frame down in the ARM-core to
# VideoCore bus translation, which is a fixed offset on 2837/2838 and a
# different one on 2712:
#
#   bcm2835_mbox_fb_get_w_h -> bcm2835_mbox_property
#     -> bcm283x_dmabus_peripheral_lowaddr -> bcm283x_armc_to_vcbus -> panic
#
# sys/conf/files.arm64 gates both files on exactly these options:
#   bcm2835_fbd.c   optional vt soc_brcm_bcm2837 fdt | vt soc_brcm_bcm2838 fdt
#   bcm2835_mbox.c  optional soc_brcm_bcm2837 fdt | soc_brcm_bcm2838 fdt
#
# so switching them off is the correct fix rather than a workaround: this
# board wants a SOC_BRCM_BCM2712 of its own (native-boot plan, phase 3), not
# the Pi 4's.
nooptions	SOC_BRCM_BCM2837
nooptions	SOC_BRCM_BCM2838

# ...but do enable this board's own SoC, which gates the PCIe host bridge.
#
# Everything useful on these boards is behind PCIe: the NVMe on pcie1, and RP1 --
# and therefore both USB controllers, and therefore the keyboard -- on pcie2.
# Without this the kernel reaches mountroot and `?` lists zero disks.
#
# The driver is FreeBSD's own bcm2838_pci, extended to match
# "brcm,bcm2712-pcie" (patches/0013). It has to be compiled in rather than
# shipped as a kext: route (a) has no loader, so nothing loads a .ko at boot,
# and a kext delivering PCIe would have to come off a disk that only exists
# once PCIe works.
options 	SOC_BRCM_BCM2712
